WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:06.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  loser  [ __ ]  [ __ ]  bullying  definitely  not a topic back in the 90s  not a topic back in the 90s in brazil  where i grew up  but violence in the school was a reality  for many of us  i remember  a classmate with a lisp  he couldn't talk pronounce a single word  without being mocked  i remember  the cross i'd grow from the school bus  who was always alone ignored by the  other girls  in the yard there was always someone who  was  too  short too fat too tall too skinny to  something that had to be pointed out  me  i was too soft  too gentle  not aggressive enough for a boy  i didn't know i was gay at the age of  seven  but bullying was already a daily task of  endurance for me  i remember  entering the school in fear  never knowing when the insults would  start  i remember spending entire school breaks  locked in restrooms to protect myself  from punches  but  when punches happen  i was the one sent to the principal's  office  i still felt my throat burning and  tightening while i tried as much as i  could to  hold back my tears  boys don't cry  be a man ricardo  what that was supposed to mean  wasn't i a man  when good friends  try to defend me against bullies they  emphatically claim things like stop  he's not gay  of course i was happy to have someone  watching my back but when i  finally realized that actually  i was gay  the whole thing made me feel  embarrassed lonely  ashamed  my identity was denied even by those who  in theory were my side my friends  back home  the fear of disappointing my parents  kept me quiet how could mommy and daddy  be proud of a [ __ ]  because i was different  i blamed myself for being bullied  i was convinced that i didn't deserve a  place in the world  for relief  from the daily bullying at school i  turned myself to the make-believe world  of movies  thank god i had movies  edward scissorhands and forrest gump  proved that outliers could also drive  bad education brokeback mountain portray  larger visions of love encouraged me to  stand tall  no matter what i had to find a way to be  happy  here i am  cinema changed my life through my  favorite characters  i leave vicariously and learn  how to overcome prejudice  when it came time to choose a career go  to the university i wanted to help other  struggling teenagers to find comfort and  acceptance  i decided to do through cinema went to  film school and soon became an  international sales agent  my job  was selling films to  theatrical distributors  tv channels  and streaming platforms across the world  by negotiating film rights i could  choose what people would watch next  embracing diversity i couldn't give a  voice for those in oppression like me  but also foster cultural exchange  it's much easier to understand another  culture  if we have seen its people on the screen  before right  i was happy to represent rafiki a queer  coming of age from kenya where  homosexuality is still a crime  or baraka meets baraka a romantic comedy  from saudi arabia calling from freedom  in the region  i was proud of myself  but  as a professional of the film industry i  had to acknowledge how problematic films  could also be  while cinema helped me and many others  to embrace our identities  it also spread the stereotype of  masculinity that not only shaped and  normalized my aggressor's behaviors but  i also encourage them to be violent and  cruel  think about our  traditional heroes  batman  james bond  or the killing machines from the 80s and  90s  rumble robocop the terminator  apart from sarcastic jokes they don't  talk much never ask or answer questions  but  nobody knows what's going on in their  heads but all of a sudden  it's violence o'clock  they express themselves sending dozens  to hell  hasta la vista baby  but wait  bad communication skills and the  tendency to solve differences with  violence doesn't that sound like  bullying  sometimes i wonder who my aggressors  back in school are victimizing today  according to these statistics  more than 85 percent of the homicides in  the u.s were committed by men  and more than two-thirds of the women  killed in the country were shot by their  husbands in cases of domestic violence  instead of encouraging boys to be kind  and vulnerable  hollywood has to stimulate them to hone  muscles and ninja skills  spreading a toxic stereotype of  masculinity  the pressure to conform has prevented  men from sharing emotions and forced  them to forego  empathy  year after year  we have seen on the screen  the same old formulas  but hollywood now is investing in  diversity  here's the question  keeping the same old models  wouldn't just perpetuate the same old  violent stereotypes  white western men are no longer the only  ones around but violent characters are  still the coolest  on the big screen  the good news  is that on smaller screens  streaming platforms offered today more  than ever content from different  countries communities  and cultures  we are a click away  from films that depicts that convey  different definitions of man different  kinds of people and different ways of  living life  imagine  if as a child my colleagues and i have  had access to all these different ways  of living life  we would probably feel less  different less lonely  i would feel that yes i was a man a more  soft and gentle one but is still a man  who deserved a place in the world  the other kids in the school would  accept not only me  but the classmates of a lisp  the crowds i had grown from the school  bus  and all the other kids in the yard who  were choose something  we are  we still have a long way to go against  more than a century  of oppressive characters in films  but the growing diversity  on the screen is a beacon of hope for a  more empathetic society  as an audience i invite you today to be  curious  to explore new narratives  and to make space for people to be just  different  no violence of any kind even is  acceptable  let's make the world a more kind place  not only for struggling kids  but for all of us  thank you  [Music]  you

